The Terraform configuration provided has a minor issue with the `data.aws_caller_identity.current` resource, which is causing a 403 error due to an invalid security token. To resolve this, we can remove the `data.aws_caller_identity.current` resource and directly use the AWS account ID in the KMS key policy. This approach avoids the need for the `data` source and should resolve the error.

Here is the corrected Terraform configuration:

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_kms_key" "s3_bucket_encryption_key" {
  description             = "KMS key for S3 bucket encryption"
  deletion_window_in_days = 10
  policy                  = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Id      = "key-default-1"
    Statement = [
      {
        Sid    = "Enable IAM User Permissions"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          AWS = "arn:aws:iam::<AWS_ACCOUNT_ID>:root"
        }
        Action   = "kms:*"
        Resource = "*"
      },
      {
        Sid    = "Allow use of the key"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "s3.amazonaws.com"
        }
        Action = [
          "kms:Encrypt",
          "kms:Decrypt",
          "kms:ReEncrypt*",
          "kms:GenerateDataKey*",
          "kms:DescribeKey"
        ]
        Resource = "*"
      }
    ]
  })
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "my_s3_bucket" {
  bucket = "my-s3-bucket-unique-name"
  acl    = "private"

  server_side_encryption_configuration {
    rule {
      apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
        sse_algorithm     = "aws:kms"
        kms_master_key_id = aws_kms_key.s3_bucket_encryption_key.arn
      }
    }
  }
}
```

In this corrected configuration:
- The `data.aws_caller_identity.current` resource has been removed.
- The AWS account ID is directly hardcoded in the KMS key policy. You should replace `<AWS_ACCOUNT_ID>` with your actual AWS account ID.

This should resolve the Terraform plan error and allow the deployment of the S3 bucket with KMS server-side encryption.

